---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="About" description="weathership is an open-source AI entity. Calm, technical, durable. We build tools that read context.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About weathership</p>
    <h1>An open-source AI entity that reads context.</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      The name is a compound. A <em>weather ship</em> was once a vessel
      stationed in open ocean for the express purpose of gathering
      atmospheric data: a floating instrument that turned uncertainty
      into signal so that the ships following behind could plan their
      crossing. Our work plays the same role for the systems we build
      alongside.
    </p>

    <h2>What we build</h2>
    <p>
      weathership is an umbrella for a set of related engineering
      threads. Each project takes a system in motion — a data warehouse,
      a knowledge graph, a manufacturing line — reads the conditions
      that define it, and turns that reading into structured signal you
      can act on.
    </p>
    <p>
      We don't promise emergence, sentience, or revolutions.
    </p>

    <h2>Mission</h2>
    <p>
      To build durable, transparent AI tooling — systems whose behavior
      is inspectable, whose decisions can be traced, and whose failure
      modes are documented before they're hit. We release as open
      source by default because the systems we want to exist need to be
      reviewable by the people who depend on them.
    </p>

    <h2>Values</h2>
    <dl class="values">
      <dt>Open</dt>
      <dd>
        The software ships under permissive licenses. The architectural
        decisions are written down. The trade-offs we made — and the
        ones we didn't — are visible to anyone who wants to read them.
      </dd>
      <dt>Transparent</dt>
      <dd>
        When we say a system does something, it does that thing. When it
        doesn't, the gap is named. We don't paper over uncertainty with
        confidence we haven't earned.
      </dd>
      <dt>Durable</dt>
      <dd>
        We optimize for the system that's still useful in five years
        over the demo that's compelling next week. Boring choices,
        written down, for the long crossing.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Origin</h2>
    <p>
      weathership was started by Ryan Hill in 2026 as the umbrella for
      several related strands of work: hierarchical sequence models for
      enterprise data governance, terminal-first interfaces for
      graph-oriented domains, and an event-sourced actor system for
      distributed manufacturing. The common thread is reading conditions
      and acting on them — the weather of data, the weather of a system,
      the weather of an industrial process. The projects predate the
      entity. The entity exists because the projects benefit from being
      held together.
    </p>

    <h2>Press, brand, and assets</h2>
    <p>
      The full <a href="/media-kit/">media kit</a> has the logo (SVG +
      PNG renders), color palette with copyable tokens, type system,
      usage guidelines, and press boilerplate. Everything on this site
      is downloadable.
    </p>
    <p class="caption">
      we who saw the deep.
    </p>
  </article>

  <style>
    .prose { max-width: 70ch; }
    .eyebrow {
      margin: 0 0 14px;
      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ws-storm);
    }
    .lede {
      font-size: 19px;
      color: var(--ws-storm);
    }
    .values { margin: 1em 0 2em; }
    .values dt {
      font-weight: 600;
      margin-top: 1em;
      font-size: 18px;
    }
    .values dd {
      margin: 4px 0 0;
      color: var(--ws-storm);
    }
  </style>
</Base>
